# mixed-parameters — some parameters carry a `mut` marker, others do not.
#
#   def f(mut c: Counter, n: int)   ->   f(&c: Counter, n: int)
#
# Before ordered choice a `param` rule had ONE shape, so a leading `mut` was
# either required on every parameter or on none:
#
#   `def f(mut c: Counter, n: int)`  ->  expected `mut`, found "n"   (0.22.0)
#
# Now the parameter list is a choice between two functions:
#
#   arg capture ps mut_param | plain_param* sep "," join ", "
#
# `mut_param` is listed first because it starts with the literal `mut`, which a
# plain parameter can never begin with here, so the order is only a convention —
# but if `plain_param` started with a bare `ident`, `mut` would be swallowed as
# a parameter NAME, so put the marked shape first.
#
# See docs/library-authoring.md ("Ordered choice") and PROP-2026-0004 UC-06.
extension txt

function def_fn
    arg literal "def"
    arg capture name ident
    arg literal "("
    arg capture ps mut_param | plain_param* sep "," join ", "
    arg literal ")"
    write `${name}(${ps})
`
end

function mut_param
    bare
    arg literal "mut"
    arg capture pname ident
    arg literal ":"
    arg capture ptype ident
    write `&${pname}: ${ptype}`
end

function plain_param
    bare
    arg capture pname ident
    arg literal ":"
    arg capture ptype ident
    write `${pname}: ${ptype}`
end
